"""Generate concise, inspectable component notes with interview practice."""
from pathlib import Path
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
TOPICS={
"01-dynamics":("Six-degree-of-freedom dynamics","MuJoCo advances one rigid body with a free joint. World z points up. Attitude is a unit quaternion in wxyz order. External force acts at the center of mass; a gimballed engine at the lower end creates torque through its physical lever. Four RK4 evaluations approximate continuous acceleration within each integration step.","m v_dot = R(q) F_body + m g + F_drag\nI omega_dot + omega cross (I omega) = tau\nq_dot = 0.5 q multiply [0, omega]","MuJoCo avoids maintaining a second hand-coded integrator for controllers versus RL. Fifty-hertz integration and ten-hertz control are explicit approximations. Changing mass requires mj_setConst, which resets qpos internally; live pose, velocity and time are restored afterward.",[
("Why six degrees of freedom?","Three translations and three attitude coordinates determine spatial rigid-body motion; the quaternion stores attitude redundantly with a unit-length constraint."),
("Why a quaternion?","It avoids Euler-angle singularities. MuJoCo uses wxyz, while Three.js uses xyzw; the renderer also changes from z-up to y-up."),
("Where does torque come from?","From r cross F at the engine lever. No artificial attitude torque is added for the RL policy."),
("How was the integrator checked?","Free fall is compared with h=h0+v0*t-0.5*g*t^2; vacuum energy and horizontal momentum are checked numerically."),
("What is missing?","Flexible-body motion, detailed aerodynamics, contact stability, slosh and center-of-mass migration. The touchdown plane is a simplified COM-height test.")]),
"02-rocket-equation":("Fuel flow and the rocket equation","Specific impulse converts propellant flow into thrust through an effective exhaust velocity. Each integration substep consumes only available propellant. The last substep caps delivered impulse so fuel cannot become negative. Mass and inertia are updated as fuel burns.","v_e = Isp * g0\nmdot = -T / (Isp * g0)\ndelta_v_ideal = Isp * g0 * ln(m_initial / m_final)","The same effective exhaust velocity appears in the thrust law and Tsiolkovsky equation. We apply thrust/m acceleration and decrement mass; we do not add a second rocket-equation acceleration, which would double-count propulsion.",[
("What does Isp measure?","Thrust divided by propellant weight flow; its units are seconds."),
("Why does acceleration increase as fuel burns?","For the same thrust, T/m grows as mass falls."),
("Why not directly add ideal delta-v each step?","Force integration already produces propulsion acceleration. Adding the ideal rocket equation separately double-counts it."),
("Does this include gravity loss?","The dynamics integrate gravity and drag separately, so actual velocity gain differs from the ideal vacuum mass-ratio formula."),
("What limits remain?","Constant Isp, simplified inertia scaling and unlimited restart commands omit pressure dependence, ignition delays and tank geometry.")]),
"03-mdp":("The Markov decision process","The agent maps a physical observation to throttle and two gimbal commands. An episode begins from a seeded distribution and ends in a specific mission outcome. The same underlying simulator is used by classical controllers. Observation noise and unobserved wind make the agent's actual information partially observable.","MDP = (S, A, P, r, gamma)\na in [-1,1]^3\npi(a | observed_state, relative_goal)","Physical-state learning isolates control from vision. The observation contains position, velocity, quaternion, angular velocity, fuel fraction, clock, relative target, target ascent velocity, mass and previous throttle. There are no controller actions or expert demonstrations.",[
("Is the observation strictly Markov?","Not entirely: wind, gimbal actuator state and sensor noise are not fully observed. The full simulator state is Markov; the policy sees a partial observation."),
("Why normalize actions?","A symmetric [-1,1] Box supports common continuous-control policies. Physics maps throttle to [0,1] and enforces an engine-on floor."),
("Why include the goal?","The same state can require different behavior for different targets. Relative position provides that task context."),
("How is combined flight handled?","A phase manager switches from ascent to descent at separation while retaining pose, velocity, mass and simulation time."),
("Does a physical-state policy understand the 3D view?","No. Browser rendering is a display of telemetry and is not part of the policy observation.")]),
"04-reward":("Reward shaping","Progress is the change in negative distance-to-go. Running costs discourage near-ground speed, tilt, angular rate, lateral error, elapsed time and fuel burn. Terminal rewards distinguish success from failure, while smooth terminal error costs make near misses informative.","r = w_progress * (Phi(s_next)-Phi(s)) - dt * running_cost - w_fuel * fuel_burn\nr_terminal += success_bonus or failure_penalty - terminal_error_cost","Every weight is in config.yaml. Initial pilots learned faster failure without mission success, prompting reduced progress weight and stronger terminal speed costs. This is an empirical design change, not evidence that the revised policy has converged. Reward versions must be recorded when comparing curves.",[
("Can shaping change the optimum?","Yes. Our difference-of-potential term omits the gamma factor used in the formal policy-invariant shaping result, and other costs intentionally change preferences."),
("How can reward be exploited?","Large progress reward may favor fast ground impact; weak time cost may favor hovering. Inspect actual termination reasons and trajectories."),
("Why smooth terminal costs?","They give near misses different feedback instead of treating every failure identically."),
("Why multiply running costs by dt?","It reduces dependence on control frequency, although discretization and termination effects still matter."),
("Does higher reward mean success?","No. Success is computed from explicit mission thresholds, independently of reward magnitude.")]),
"05-curriculum":("Curriculum learning","Training starts with a narrow position, velocity and tilt distribution and reduced disturbances. A rolling window tracks actual episode success. After the threshold is passed, difficulty widens until the full distribution is reached. Development evaluation always uses the full distribution.","advance if successes_in_window / window_size >= 0.75\nwindow_size = 40\ndifficulty_next = min(1, difficulty + 0.15)","This keeps advancement tied to measured competence. Four vectorized environments collect experience; DummyVecEnv steps them sequentially in one process. An agent that never succeeds stays at its current curriculum stage, even if reward improves.",[
("Why not start with the hardest distribution?","Sparse success and unstable attitude may prevent useful early learning."),
("What triggers advancement?","At least 30 actual successes in the latest 40 training episodes, using the configured threshold."),
("Can curriculum performance be reported as full-task reliability?","No. The full start distribution, noise and wind must be evaluated separately."),
("What if the agent forgets earlier stages?","The current implementation widens a nested distribution; retention still needs evaluation. It does not guarantee immunity to forgetting."),
("Is vectorization parallel simulation?","Not here. Four DummyVecEnv instances batch policy calls but step physics sequentially, avoiding Windows spawn memory overhead.")]),
"06-ppo":("Proximal policy optimization","PPO alternates policy rollouts and several minibatch optimization epochs. A probability ratio compares the new policy with the policy that collected the rollout. Clipping discourages excessively large updates. A learned value baseline reduces policy-gradient variance.","ratio = pi_new(a|s) / pi_old(a|s)\nL_clip = E[min(ratio*A, clip(ratio,1-eps,1+eps)*A)]\ndelta_t = r_t + gamma*V(s_next) - V(s)\nA_GAE = sum_l (gamma*lambda)^l * delta_(t+l)","SB3 supplies the tested algorithm. This project uses a small MLP, normalized observations/rewards and a fixed seed. MLP PPO defaults to CPU because the environment loop and small batch transfers often dominate. Its final step count can exceed the budget by a rollout.",[
("What does clipping accomplish?","It removes the incentive to keep increasing the surrogate objective after a ratio exceeds the configured interval in the advantageous direction; it is not a hard KL constraint."),
("What is GAE?","A discounted sum of temporal-difference residuals controlled by lambda, trading bias against variance."),
("Why entropy regularization?","It rewards policy spread and can prevent premature collapse, but too much exploration harms precise control."),
("Why save VecNormalize?","Network inputs depend on running statistics. Replaying weights with different statistics changes behavior."),
("Why does CPU sometimes beat CUDA?","A tiny MLP may spend more time transferring data and waiting on CPU simulation than doing matrix arithmetic.")]),
"07-sac":("Soft actor-critic","SAC reuses experience from a replay buffer. Two critic networks estimate action values, and the actor optimizes expected value plus entropy. Target critics are softly updated to stabilize bootstrapping. The temperature controls the exploration-reward tradeoff.","J_pi = E[alpha*log pi(a|s) - min(Q1(s,a),Q2(s,a))]\ny = r + gamma*(min(Q1_target,Q2_target) - alpha*log pi(a_next|s_next))\nQ_target <- tau*Q + (1-tau)*Q_target","The candidate uses the same observations, actions and physics as PPO. CUDA is supported and was used on the verified RTX 5070 Ti. Evaluation loads only the policy/critics with a one-transition placeholder replay buffer, avoiding an unnecessary full training buffer allocation.",[
("Why twin critics?","Using the minimum of two estimates mitigates optimistic value-estimation bias, though it can introduce pessimism."),
("Why off-policy?","Replay allows several updates from earlier experience and improves sample reuse."),
("What is the entropy temperature?","Alpha weighs stochasticity relative to reward; automatic tuning adjusts it toward a target entropy."),
("Can this project exactly resume SAC?","Weights and normalization are saved, but replay-buffer persistence and exact resumed equivalence are not yet implemented."),
("Is SAC automatically better than PPO?","No. Compare actual held-out success, robustness and compute cost at matching distributions and budgets.")]),
"08-pid-lqr":("Feedback control and LQR","The untuned baseline uses weak altitude and attitude feedback. Tuned feedback tracks an altitude-dependent descent-speed reference. Engine gimbal couples lateral translation and attitude, and its initial lateral force can oppose the eventual desired correction. The final version models this chain with an LQR regulator.","PID: u = Kp*e + Ki*integral(e dt) + Kd*e_dot\nLQR: minimize integral(x^T Q x + u^T R u) dt\nA^T P + P A - P B R^-1 B^T P + Q = 0\nu = -R^-1 B^T P x","These controllers use a PD subset of the general PID family; no integral term is claimed. The LQR state is lateral position, velocity, body-axis tilt and tilt rate. Vertical feedforward differentiates the descent speed reference, reducing touchdown lag. Saturation and gimbal rate limits remain in physics.",[
("Is the implemented baseline a full PID?","It is PID-style cascaded feedback with proportional and derivative terms, no integral accumulator. The documentation states that explicitly."),
("Why can cascaded loops oscillate?","Attitude lag and adverse initial gimbal translation add phase delay to lateral feedback."),
("What does LQR optimize?","A quadratic state-and-input cost for a local linear model, not global fuel optimality for the nonlinear mission."),
("Why use feedforward?","Tracking a changing velocity reference requires its expected acceleration as well as feedback on velocity error."),
("What happens under actuator saturation?","The LQR assumptions no longer hold exactly. The physical simulator clips gimbal/thrust and evaluation exposes resulting failures.")]),
"09-convex-guidance":("Convex powered-descent approximation","A finite-horizon trajectory uses position, velocity and acceleration variables. Linear discrete dynamics, an upper thrust norm bound and an approximate pointing cone form a second-order cone problem. CLARABEL solves it; an attitude controller tracks the resulting reference.","p_(k+1) = p_k + v_k*dt + 0.5*a_k*dt^2\nv_(k+1) = v_k + a_k*dt\n||a_k - g|| <= T_max / m_assumed\nminimize sum ||a_k||^2 + small_weight*sum ||v_k||^2","Constant mass keeps the problem convex and inspectable. A positive lower thrust norm is nonconvex and is relaxed in planning; physics still enforces it. Reference tracking has real attitude lag. Solver status is recorded, and a documented feedback fallback handles infeasibility.",[
("Is this lossless convexification?","No. It is a simplified constant-mass convex approximation, without a proof that a relaxation recovers the original optimum."),
("Why is minimum thrust hard?","The set outside a norm ball is nonconvex, while an upper norm bound defines a convex set."),
("Does a feasible plan guarantee successful flight?","No. Mass changes, gimbal rate limits, attitude tracking, disturbances and approximation error can invalidate it."),
("Is the objective fuel optimal?","It minimizes squared acceleration plus a velocity term. It is not an exact propellant-minimization formulation."),
("What happens if the solver fails?","The run retains a solver-status label and uses the feedback controller. Results must not call that flight an optimal-solver success.")]),
"10-randomization":("Domain randomization and robustness","Seeded resets vary vehicle mass and thrust scale. Wind follows a mean-reverting gust process, air density decays exponentially with altitude, and the observation has configurable sensor noise. Curriculum scales disturbances during training; full evaluations use the complete distribution.","d wind = k*(mean-wind)*dt + sigma*sqrt(dt)*normal(0,1)\nrho(h) = rho0*exp(-h/H)\nF_drag = -0.5*rho*Cd*A*||v-wind||*(v-wind)","Randomization reduces dependence on one ideal model. It does not prove transfer to reality. Robustness scenarios separately increase gusts and noise, reduce thrust, and widen initial conditions. Failure counts are reported per scenario rather than blended into a single flattering number.",[
("Why seed the gust process?","Matching seeds and action histories produces reproducible disturbances and directly comparable experiments."),
("Are wind histories exactly identical across controllers?","The same random process is used on a common fixed integration clock, but different episode durations and control histories can change the observed prefix/termination."),
("Does randomization guarantee sim-to-real transfer?","No. Unmodeled effects and incorrect parameter ranges can still dominate."),
("What information advantage does classical control have?","Its modeled drag feedforward uses idealized wind information, while RL observes physical kinematics rather than explicit wind. Same physics does not mean identical estimator information."),
("Why test thrust loss separately?","A controller can handle nominal randomization yet fail under a systematic engine degradation outside the training range.")]),
"11-statistics":("Evaluation statistics","Each evaluation runs a stated seed interval and records every terminal outcome. Success rate is a binomial estimate, not a guarantee. A Wilson interval provides uncertainty even when all sampled flights succeed or none do. Speed, tilt, offset and fuel are reported with mean and median.","p_hat = k/n\ncenter = (p_hat + z^2/(2n))/(1+z^2/n)\nhalfwidth = z*sqrt(p_hat*(1-p_hat)/n + z^2/(4n^2))/(1+z^2/n)\nz = 1.95996398454","Development seeds are for checkpoint selection; the default full qualification pool is separate. Pilot samples are labeled and cannot close the 10,000-seed gate. Touchdown_speed is total terminal speed; for takeoff it is separation speed and must not be misread as impact speed.",[
("Why a confidence interval?","Finite samples leave uncertainty about the true probability under the modeled distribution."),
("Why Wilson rather than a simple normal interval?","Wilson behaves better near zero or all-success counts and avoids a zero-width certainty claim."),
("Does 10,000 successes prove no failures?","No. It gives a strong but finite sample result with a nonzero uncertainty bound and says nothing about unmodeled scenarios."),
("Why use disjoint development/test seeds?","Selecting the best checkpoint on the same seeds later reported as independent evidence introduces selection bias."),
("What does same-seed comparison buy?","Pairing starts and disturbances reduces variation unrelated to controller choice; it does not remove model bias.")]),
"12-comparison":("Why RL and classical control differ","Classical guidance encodes physical structure and can work before collecting training episodes. RL optimizes a parameterized policy through sampled rewards and can learn behaviors absent from a hand-designed law. Both remain limited by their model, observation and actuator assumptions.","classical: state -> explicit guidance law -> actuator command\nRL: state -> learned neural policy -> actuator command\ncomparison: same dynamics, start seeds, outcome thresholds and scenario","The project preserves failed runs and real learning curves. A reward increase without success is learning progress only in the reward objective. Four named replay slots are a storytelling interface, not automatic evidence of four maturity stages. Algorithm choice should be based on held-out success and robustness at an explicit compute budget.",[
("Why can classical win with little data?","It exploits known mechanics and design insight; its engineering effort is not counted as training samples."),
("Why might RL eventually improve?","It may optimize nonlinear or coupled strategies across many samples, provided the observation/reward and coverage support them."),
("Is this a perfectly fair information comparison?","Physics and seeds match, but classical modeled wind feedforward is an information advantage. Report it rather than obscuring it."),
("What qualifies a Final level?","A label alone does not. Held-out success, uncertainty, robustness and independent qualification are needed."),
("Would you fly this controller on hardware?","No. This educational simulator omits many flight-critical effects and has no certification, hardware-in-loop or safety case.")]),
}
def main():
    folder=ROOT/"docs";folder.mkdir(exist_ok=True)
    links=[]
    for slug,(title,what,formula,why,questions) in TOPICS.items():
        text=f"# {title}\n\n## What it does\n\n{what}\n\n## Why this approach\n\n{why}\n\n## Key formulas\n\n```text\n{formula}\n```\n\n## Five interview questions\n\n"
        for i,(q,a) in enumerate(questions,1):text+=f"{i}. **{q}** {a}\n\n"
        text+="## Evidence and further reading\n\nSee [research sources](RESEARCH.md), the relevant implementation in `rocket_lab/`, and the actual validation record in [VALIDATION.md](VALIDATION.md).\n"
        (folder/f"{slug}.md").write_text(text,encoding="utf-8");links.append(f"- [{title}]({slug}.md)")
    (folder/"README.md").write_text("# Flight manual\n\n"+"\n".join(links)+"\n\n- [Research sources](RESEARCH.md)\n- [Validation record](VALIDATION.md)\n",encoding="utf-8")
if __name__=="__main__":main()
